    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-360ve",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "360VE Time for payment of damages",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-act-1997#sec-360ve"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-act-1997.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-act-1997.pdf, p.248",
      "values:originalText": "Decision whether to accept liability for damages\n\n(1) If, at a particular time, a statutory infrastructure provider first has\nreason to believe that an event has occurred that is reasonably\nlikely to result in the statutory infrastructure provider being liable\nto pay damages to a particular customer under section 360VD, the\nstatutory infrastructure provider must:\n\n(a) decide whether to accept that liability; and\n\n(b) do so:\n\n(i) within 14 days after that time; or\n\n(ii) if another number of days is the relevant number of days\nfor the purposes of the application of this subsection to\nthe designated compensable rule or designated\ncompensable standard to which the liability relates—\nwithin the relevant number of days after that time.\n\n(2) In making a decision under subsection (1), the statutory\ninfrastructure provider must have regard to whether there is any\nreasonable basis for the statutory infrastructure provider to dispute\nthe liability.\n\n(3) If a statutory infrastructure provider makes a decision under\nsubsection (1) not to accept a liability to pay damages to a\nparticular customer, the statutory infrastructure provider must give\nthe customer written notification of the decision within 14 weeks\nafter the decision is made.\nCrediting customer account\n\n(4) If:\n\n(a) a statutory infrastructure provider makes a decision under\nsubsection (1) to accept a liability to pay damages to a\nparticular customer; and\n\n(b) the liability is to be discharged by giving the customer a\ncredit in an account the customer has with the statutory\ninfrastructure provider;\n\n\nStatutory infrastructure providers  Part 19\nCompensation  Division 4A\n\nSection 360VF\n\nTelecommunications Act 1997\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\nthe liability must be discharged within the period of 14 weeks after\nthe decision is made and:\n\n(c) if it is practicable for the statutory infrastructure provider to\ngive the customer the credit within that 14-week period and\nin time for the customer to be notified of the credit in the first\nbill sent to the customer during that period—by giving the\ncustomer the credit in time for the customer to be notified of\nthe credit in that bill; or\n\n(d) if paragraph (c) does not apply, but it is practicable for the\nstatutory infrastructure provider to give the customer the\ncredit within that 14-week period and in time for the\ncustomer to be notified of the credit in the second bill sent to\nthe customer during that period—by giving the customer the\ncredit in time for the customer to be notified of the credit in\nthat bill.\nOther manner of discharging liability\n\n(5) If:\n\n(a) a statutory infrastructure provider makes a decision under\nsubsection (1) to accept a liability to pay damages to a\nparticular customer; and\n\n(b) the liability is not to be discharged by giving the customer a\ncredit in an account the customer has with the statutory\ninfrastructure provider;\nthe liability must be discharged within 14 weeks after the decision\nis made.\nCustomer\n\n(6) If the customer is an individual and the customer dies, a reference\nin this section to the customer includes a reference to the legal\npersonal representative of the customer.",
      "skos:definition": "Decision whether to accept liability for damages\n\n(1) If, at a particular time, a statutory infrastructure provider first has\nreason to believe that an event has occurred that is reasonably\nlikely to result in the statutory infrastructure provider being liable\nto pay damages to a particular customer under section 360VD, the\nstatutory infrastructure provider must:\n\n(a) decide whether to accept that liability; and\n\n(b) do so:\n\n(i) within 14 days after that time; or\n\n(ii) if another number of days is the relevant number of days\nfor the purposes of the application of this subsection to\nthe designated compensable rule or designated\ncompensable standard to which the liability relates—\nwithin the relevant number of days after that time.\n\n(2) In making a decision under subsection (1), the statutory\ninfrastructure provider must have regard to whether there is any\nreasonable basis for the statutory infrastructure provider to dispute\nthe liability.\n\n(3) If a statutory infrastructure provider makes a decision under\nsubsection (1) not to accept a liability to pay damages to a\nparticular customer, the statutory infrastructure provider must give\nthe customer written notification of the decision within 14 weeks\nafter the decision is made.\nCrediting customer account\n\n(4) If:\n\n(a) a statutory infrastructure provider makes a decision under\nsubsection (1) to accept a liability to pay damages to a\nparticular customer; and\n\n(b) the liability is to be discharged by giving the customer a\ncredit in an account the customer has with the statutory\ninfrastructure provider;\n\n\nStatutory infrastructure providers  Part 19\nCompensation  Division 4A\n\nSection 360VF\n\nTelecommunications Act 1997\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\nthe liability must be discharged within the period of 14 weeks after\nthe decision is made and:\n\n(c) if it is practicable for the statutory infrastructure provider to\ngive the customer the credit within that 14-week period and\nin time for the customer to be notified of the credit in",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-360ve-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-360ve-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-360ve-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-360ve-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-360ve-ss-5"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-360ve-ss-6"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "9c479f80ffb0efdb70fc128ef7030c067809250b46fc845777533848d18e3ec7"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-act-1997#sec-360ve",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "360VE Time for payment of damages",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 248,
      "values:originalText": "Decision whether to accept liability for damages\n\n(1) If, at a particular time, a statutory infrastructure provider first has\nreason to believe that an event has occurred that is reasonably\nlikely to result in the statutory infrastructure provider being liable\nto pay damages to a particular customer under section 360VD, the\nstatutory infrastructure provider must:\n\n(a) decide whether to accept that liability; and\n\n(b) do so:\n\n(i) within 14 days after that time; or\n\n(ii) if another number of days is the relevant number of days\nfor the purposes of the application of this subsection to\nthe designated compensable rule or designated\ncompensable standard to which the liability relates—\nwithin the relevant number of days after that time.\n\n(2) In making a decision under subsection (1), the statutory\ninfrastructure provider must have regard to whether there is any\nreasonable basis for the statutory infrastructure provider to dispute\nthe liability.\n\n(3) If a statutory infrastructure provider makes a decision under\nsubsection (1) not to accept a liability to pay damages to a\nparticular customer, the statutory infrastructure provider must give\nthe customer written notification of the decision within 14 weeks\nafter the decision is made.\nCrediting customer account\n\n(4) If:\n\n(a) a statutory infrastructure provider makes a decision under\nsubsection (1) to accept a liability to pay damages to a\nparticular customer; and\n\n(b) the liability is to be discharged by giving the customer a\ncredit in an account the customer has with the statutory\ninfrastructure provider;\n\n\nStatutory infrastructure providers  Part 19\nCompensation  Division 4A\n\nSection 360VF\n\nTelecommunications Act 1997\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\nthe liability must be discharged within the period of 14 weeks after\nthe decision is made and:\n\n(c) if it is practicable for the statutory infrastructure provider to\ngive the customer the credit within that 14-week period and\nin time for the customer to be notified of the credit in the first\nbill sent to the customer during that period—by giving the\ncustomer the credit in time for the customer to be notified of\nthe credit in that bill; or\n\n(d) if paragraph (c) does not apply, but it is practicable for the\nstatutory infrastructure provider to give the customer the\ncredit within that 14-week period and in time for the\ncustomer to be notified of the credit in the second bill sent to\nthe customer during that period—by giving the customer the\ncredit in time for the customer to be notified of the credit in\nthat bill.\nOther manner of discharging liability\n\n(5) If:\n\n(a) a statutory infrastructure provider makes a decision under\nsubsection (1) to accept a liability to pay damages to a\nparticular customer; and\n\n(b) the liability is not to be discharged by giving the customer a\ncredit in an account the customer has with the statutory\ninfrastructure provider;\nthe liability must be discharged within 14 weeks after the decision\nis made.\nCustomer\n\n(6) If the customer is an individual and the customer dies, a reference\nin this section to the customer includes a reference to the legal\npersonal representative of the customer."
    },
