    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-373",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "373 Simplified outline",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-act-1997#sec-373"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-act-1997.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-act-1997.pdf, p.310",
      "values:originalText": "The following is a simplified outline of this Part:\n•\nThe ACMA may make the following types of standards:\n\n(a)\ntechnical standards about customer equipment and\ncustomer cabling;\n\n(b)\nstandards relating to the features of customer equipment\nthat are designed to cater for the special needs of\npersons with disabilities;\n\n(c)\ntechnical standards about the interconnection of\nfacilities;\n\n(d)\ntechnical standards relating to Layer 2 bitstream\nservices.\n•\nThe ACMA may require customer equipment and customer\ncabling to be labelled so as to indicate compliance with\nstandards.\n•\nThe ACMA may issue connection permits, and make\nconnection rules, authorising the connection of customer\nequipment and customer cabling that does not comply with\nthe labelling requirements.\n•\nA label may include a compliance symbol. The unauthorised\nuse of compliance symbols is prohibited.\n•\nThe ACMA may grant cabling licences, and make cabling\nprovider rules, authorising the performance of cabling work.\n•\nCivil actions may be instituted for unlawful or dangerous\nconnections of customer equipment or customer cabling.\n\n\nTechnical regulation  Part 21\nSimplified outline  Division 1\n\nSection 373\n\nTelecommunications Act 1997\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n•\nDangerous equipment and cabling may be disconnected from\nnetworks.\n•\nThe ACMA may prohibit the supply or possession of\ndangerous equipment or cabling.",
      "skos:definition": "The following is a simplified outline of this Part:\n•\nThe ACMA may make the following types of standards:\n\n(a)\ntechnical standards about customer equipment and\ncustomer cabling;\n\n(b)\nstandards relating to the features of customer equipment\nthat are designed to cater for the special needs of\npersons with disabilities;\n\n(c)\ntechnical standards about the interconnection of\nfacilities;\n\n(d)\ntechnical standards relating to Layer 2 bitstream\nservices.\n•\nThe ACMA may require customer equipment and customer\ncabling to be labelled so as to indicate compliance with\nstandards.\n•\nThe ACMA may issue connection permits, and make\nconnection rules, authorising the connection of customer\nequipment and customer cabling that does not comply with\nthe labelling requirements.\n•\nA label may include a compliance symbol. The unauthorised\nuse of compliance symbols is prohibited.\n•\nThe ACMA may grant cabling licences, and make cabling\nprovider rules, authorising the performance of cabling work.\n•\nCivil actions may be instituted for unlawful or dangerous\nconnections of customer equipment or customer cabling.\n\n\nTechnical regulation  Part 21\nSimplified outline  Division 1\n\nSection 373\n\nTelecommunications Act 1997\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n•\nDangerous equipment and cabling may be disconnected from\nnetworks.\n•\nThe ACMA may prohibit the supply or possession of\ndangerous equipment or cabling.",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-373-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-373-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "76971b4e9a74c0e7afb743aeb7fd75bf9eb7e5b6754c46a322bccc3398a26bc9",
      "skos:note": "ACMA may prohibit supply or possession of dangerous equipment or cabling."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-act-1997#sec-373",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "373 Simplified outline",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 310,
      "values:originalText": "The following is a simplified outline of this Part:\n•\nThe ACMA may make the following types of standards:\n\n(a)\ntechnical standards about customer equipment and\ncustomer cabling;\n\n(b)\nstandards relating to the features of customer equipment\nthat are designed to cater for the special needs of\npersons with disabilities;\n\n(c)\ntechnical standards about the interconnection of\nfacilities;\n\n(d)\ntechnical standards relating to Layer 2 bitstream\nservices.\n•\nThe ACMA may require customer equipment and customer\ncabling to be labelled so as to indicate compliance with\nstandards.\n•\nThe ACMA may issue connection permits, and make\nconnection rules, authorising the connection of customer\nequipment and customer cabling that does not comply with\nthe labelling requirements.\n•\nA label may include a compliance symbol. The unauthorised\nuse of compliance symbols is prohibited.\n•\nThe ACMA may grant cabling licences, and make cabling\nprovider rules, authorising the performance of cabling work.\n•\nCivil actions may be instituted for unlawful or dangerous\nconnections of customer equipment or customer cabling.\n\n\nTechnical regulation  Part 21\nSimplified outline  Division 1\n\nSection 373\n\nTelecommunications Act 1997\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n•\nDangerous equipment and cabling may be disconnected from\nnetworks.\n•\nThe ACMA may prohibit the supply or possession of\ndangerous equipment or cabling."
    },
