    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-414",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "414 Applying labels before satisfying requirements under",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-act-1997#sec-414"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-act-1997.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-act-1997.pdf, p.342",
      "values:originalText": "subsection 408(5)\n\n(1) If a person is subject to requirements that:\n\n(a) have been specified under subsection 408(5); and\n\n\nTechnical regulation  Part 21\nLabelling of customer equipment and customer cabling  Division 7\n\nSection 415\n\nTelecommunications Act 1997\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(b) must be met before applying a particular label to customer\nequipment or customer cabling;\nthe person must not apply:\n\n(c) the label; or\n\n(d) a label that purports to be such a label;\nbefore the person satisfies those requirements.\n\n(2) A person who contravenes subsection (1) commits an offence\npunishable on conviction by a fine not exceeding 100 penalty units.\nNote 1:\nSee also sections 4AA and 4B of the Crimes Act 1914.\nNote 2:\nSee also Division 13 of this Part (which deals with the payment of\npenalties as an alternative to prosecution).\n\n(3) Subsection (2) does not apply if the person has a reasonable\nexcuse.\nNote:\nA defendant bears an evidential burden in relation to the matter in\nsubsection (3) (see subsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code).\n415  Failure to retain records etc.\n\n(1) If the ACMA makes an instrument under subsection 407(1) that\nspecifies requirements to be met after a label has been applied, a\nmanufacturer or importer must not contravene those requirements.\n\n(2) A person commits an offence if:\n\n(a) the person is a manufacturer or importer of customer\nequipment or customer cabling; and\n\n(b) the person engages in conduct; and\n\n(c) the person’s conduct contravenes a requirement referred to in\nsubsection (1).\nPenalty: 100 penalty units.\nNote 1:\nSee also sections 4AA and 4B of the Crimes Act 1914.\nNote 2:\nSee also Division 13 of this Part (which deals with the payment of\npenalties as an alternative to prosecution).\n\n(3) Subsection (1) does not apply if the person has a reasonable\nexcuse.",
      "skos:definition": "subsection 408(5)\n\n(1) If a person is subject to requirements that:\n\n(a) have been specified under subsection 408(5); and\n\n\nTechnical regulation  Part 21\nLabelling of customer equipment and customer cabling  Division 7\n\nSection 415\n\nTelecommunications Act 1997\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(b) must be met before applying a particular label to customer\nequipment or customer cabling;\nthe person must not apply:\n\n(c) the label; or\n\n(d) a label that purports to be such a label;\nbefore the person satisfies those requirements.\n\n(2) A person who contravenes subsection (1) commits an offence\npunishable on conviction by a fine not exceeding 100 penalty units.\nNote 1:\nSee also sections 4AA and 4B of the Crimes Act 1914.\nNote 2:\nSee also Division 13 of this Part (which deals with the payment of\npenalties as an alternative to prosecution).\n\n(3) Subsection (2) does not apply if the person has a reasonable\nexcuse.\nNote:\nA defendant bears an evidential burden in relation to the matter in\nsubsection (3) (see subsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code).\n415  Failure to retain records etc.\n\n(1) If the ACMA makes an instrument under subsection 407(1) that\nspecifies requirements to be met after a label has been applied, a\nmanufacturer or importer must not contravene those requirements.\n\n(2) A person commits an offence if:\n\n(a) the person is a manufacturer or importer of customer\nequipment or customer cabling; and\n\n(b) the person engages in conduct; and\n\n(c) the person’s conduct contravenes a requirement referred to in\nsubsection (1).\nPenalty: 100 penalty units.\nNote 1:\nSee also sections 4AA and 4B of the Crimes Act 1914.\nNote 2:\nSee also Division 13 of this Part (which deals with the payment of\npenalties as an alternative to prosecution).\n\n(3) Subsection (1) does not apply if the person has a reasonable\nexcuse.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-414-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-414-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-414-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-414-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-414-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-414-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "8386b8e187f31c44cbafabe7112a80d7ba96d36492a0218581b1896e91c9e55e"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-act-1997#sec-414",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "414 Applying labels before satisfying requirements under",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 342,
      "values:originalText": "subsection 408(5)\n\n(1) If a person is subject to requirements that:\n\n(a) have been specified under subsection 408(5); and\n\n\nTechnical regulation  Part 21\nLabelling of customer equipment and customer cabling  Division 7\n\nSection 415\n\nTelecommunications Act 1997\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(b) must be met before applying a particular label to customer\nequipment or customer cabling;\nthe person must not apply:\n\n(c) the label; or\n\n(d) a label that purports to be such a label;\nbefore the person satisfies those requirements.\n\n(2) A person who contravenes subsection (1) commits an offence\npunishable on conviction by a fine not exceeding 100 penalty units.\nNote 1:\nSee also sections 4AA and 4B of the Crimes Act 1914.\nNote 2:\nSee also Division 13 of this Part (which deals with the payment of\npenalties as an alternative to prosecution).\n\n(3) Subsection (2) does not apply if the person has a reasonable\nexcuse.\nNote:\nA defendant bears an evidential burden in relation to the matter in\nsubsection (3) (see subsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code).\n415  Failure to retain records etc.\n\n(1) If the ACMA makes an instrument under subsection 407(1) that\nspecifies requirements to be met after a label has been applied, a\nmanufacturer or importer must not contravene those requirements.\n\n(2) A person commits an offence if:\n\n(a) the person is a manufacturer or importer of customer\nequipment or customer cabling; and\n\n(b) the person engages in conduct; and\n\n(c) the person’s conduct contravenes a requirement referred to in\nsubsection (1).\nPenalty: 100 penalty units.\nNote 1:\nSee also sections 4AA and 4B of the Crimes Act 1914.\nNote 2:\nSee also Division 13 of this Part (which deals with the payment of\npenalties as an alternative to prosecution).\n\n(3) Subsection (1) does not apply if the person has a reasonable\nexcuse."
    },
