    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-511",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "511 Preliminary inquiries",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-act-1997#sec-511"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-act-1997.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-act-1997.pdf, p.439",
      "values:originalText": "If a complaint has been made to the ACMA under section 509, the\nACMA may make inquiries of the respondent for the purposes of\ndetermining:\n\n(a) whether the ACMA has power to investigate the matter to\nwhich the complaint relates; or\n\n(b) whether the ACMA should, in its discretion, investigate the\nmatter.",
      "skos:definition": "If a complaint has been made to the ACMA under section 509, the\nACMA may make inquiries of the respondent for the purposes of\ndetermining:\n\n(a) whether the ACMA has power to investigate the matter to\nwhich the complaint relates; or\n\n(b) whether the ACMA should, in its discretion, investigate the\nmatter.",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-511-norm"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "d509a6c4b62153d324f88ce8cd4f1500a8d149ca60124c9cea9652611825ce2c",
      "skos:note": "Inquiries of respondent for determining ACMA's power to investigate"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-act-1997#sec-511",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "511 Preliminary inquiries",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 439,
      "values:originalText": "If a complaint has been made to the ACMA under section 509, the\nACMA may make inquiries of the respondent for the purposes of\ndetermining:\n\n(a) whether the ACMA has power to investigate the matter to\nwhich the complaint relates; or\n\n(b) whether the ACMA should, in its discretion, investigate the\nmatter."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-511-norm",
      "@type": "values:Obligation",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-511"
      },
      "values:deonticStatus": {
        "@id": "values:HeuristicDerived"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "values:borneBy": {
        "@id": "values:Agent"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-512",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "512 Conduct of investigations",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-act-1997#sec-512"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-act-1997.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-act-1997.pdf, p.439",
      "values:originalText": "(1) Before beginning an investigation of a matter to which a complaint\nrelates, the ACMA must inform the respondent that the matter is to\nbe investigated.\n\n(1A) However, the ACMA is not required to inform the respondent that\nthe matter is to be investigated if:\n\n(a) the matter relates to a possible breach of:\n\n(i) the Spam Act 2003 or regulations under that Act; or\n\n(ii) the Do Not Call Register Act 2006 or regulations under\nthat Act; and\n\n(b) the ACMA has reasonable grounds to believe that informing\nthe respondent is likely to result in the concealment, loss or\ndestruction of a thing connected with the breach.\n\n(2) An investigation under this Part is to be conducted as the ACMA\nthinks fit.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) Before beginning an investigation of a matter to which a complaint\nrelates, the ACMA must inform the respondent that the matter is to\nbe investigated.\n\n(1A) However, the ACMA is not required to inform the respondent that\nthe matter is to be investigated if:\n\n(a) the matter relates to a possible breach of:\n\n(i) the Spam Act 2003 or regulations under that Act; or\n\n(ii) the Do Not Call Register Act 2006 or regulations under\nthat Act; and\n\n(b) the ACMA has reasonable grounds to believe that informing\nthe respondent is likely to result in the concealment, loss or\ndestruction of a thing connected with the breach.\n\n(2) An investigation under this Part is to be conducted as the ACMA\nthinks fit.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-512-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-512-ss-1a"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-512-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "d476ffaba0d8451646a6c794dbd298aa65195b9df43a82f3bc1accdebd2d0937"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-act-1997#sec-512",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "512 Conduct of investigations",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 439,
      "values:originalText": "(1) Before beginning an investigation of a matter to which a complaint\nrelates, the ACMA must inform the respondent that the matter is to\nbe investigated.\n\n(1A) However, the ACMA is not required to inform the respondent that\nthe matter is to be investigated if:\n\n(a) the matter relates to a possible breach of:\n\n(i) the Spam Act 2003 or regulations under that Act; or\n\n(ii) the Do Not Call Register Act 2006 or regulations under\nthat Act; and\n\n(b) the ACMA has reasonable grounds to believe that informing\nthe respondent is likely to result in the concealment, loss or\ndestruction of a thing connected with the breach.\n\n(2) An investigation under this Part is to be conducted as the ACMA\nthinks fit."
    },
