    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-513",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "513 Complainant and certain other persons to be informed of",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-act-1997#sec-513"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-act-1997.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-act-1997.pdf, p.440",
      "values:originalText": "various matters\n\n(1) If the ACMA decides not to investigate, or not to investigate\nfurther, a matter to which a complaint relates, it must, as soon as\npracticable and in such manner as it thinks fit, inform the\ncomplainant and the respondent of the decision and of the reasons\nfor the decision.\n\n(2) However, the ACMA is not required to inform the respondent of\nthe decision and of the reasons for the decision if:\n\n(a) the matter relates to a possible breach of:\n\n(i) the Spam Act 2003 or regulations under that Act; or\n\n\nInvestigations  Part 26\n\nSection 514\n\nTelecommunications Act 1997\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(ii) the Do Not Call Register Act 2006 or regulations under\nthat Act; and\n\n(b) the ACMA has reasonable grounds to believe that informing\nthe respondent is likely to result in the concealment, loss or\ndestruction of a thing connected with the breach.",
      "skos:definition": "various matters\n\n(1) If the ACMA decides not to investigate, or not to investigate\nfurther, a matter to which a complaint relates, it must, as soon as\npracticable and in such manner as it thinks fit, inform the\ncomplainant and the respondent of the decision and of the reasons\nfor the decision.\n\n(2) However, the ACMA is not required to inform the respondent of\nthe decision and of the reasons for the decision if:\n\n(a) the matter relates to a possible breach of:\n\n(i) the Spam Act 2003 or regulations under that Act; or\n\n\nInvestigations  Part 26\n\nSection 514\n\nTelecommunications Act 1997\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(ii) the Do Not Call Register Act 2006 or regulations under\nthat Act; and\n\n(b) the ACMA has reasonable grounds to believe that informing\nthe respondent is likely to result in the concealment, loss or\ndestruction of a thing connected with the breach.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-513-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-513-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "4122b2a3a635adead85730721168b768180c809db2990ac4a9cc9aa3b41501c9"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-act-1997#sec-513",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "513 Complainant and certain other persons to be informed of",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 440,
      "values:originalText": "various matters\n\n(1) If the ACMA decides not to investigate, or not to investigate\nfurther, a matter to which a complaint relates, it must, as soon as\npracticable and in such manner as it thinks fit, inform the\ncomplainant and the respondent of the decision and of the reasons\nfor the decision.\n\n(2) However, the ACMA is not required to inform the respondent of\nthe decision and of the reasons for the decision if:\n\n(a) the matter relates to a possible breach of:\n\n(i) the Spam Act 2003 or regulations under that Act; or\n\n\nInvestigations  Part 26\n\nSection 514\n\nTelecommunications Act 1997\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(ii) the Do Not Call Register Act 2006 or regulations under\nthat Act; and\n\n(b) the ACMA has reasonable grounds to believe that informing\nthe respondent is likely to result in the concealment, loss or\ndestruction of a thing connected with the breach."
    },
