    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-546",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "546 Retention of things seized",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-act-1997#sec-546"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-act-1997.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-act-1997.pdf, p.469",
      "values:originalText": "(1) If an inspector seizes a thing under this Division, the inspector or\nthe ACMA may retain it until:\n\n(a) the end of the period of 60 days after the seizure; or\n\n(b) if either of the following proceedings are instituted within\nthat period:\n\n(i) proceedings for an offence against, or arising out of, this\nAct in respect of which the thing may afford evidence;\n\n(ii) proceedings for a breach of the Spam Act 2003 in\nrespect of which the thing may afford evidence;\n\nthe proceedings (including any appeal to a court in relation to\nthose proceedings) are completed.\n\n(2) The ACMA may, by written instrument, authorise a thing seized\nunder this Division to be released to the owner, or to the person\nfrom whom it was seized, either:\n\n(a) unconditionally; or\n\n(b) on such conditions as the ACMA thinks fit, including\nconditions as to giving security for payment of its value if it\nis forfeited under section 551.\n\n(3) In this section:\nthis Act includes the Telecommunications (Consumer Protection\nand Service Standards) Act 1999 and regulations under that Act.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) If an inspector seizes a thing under this Division, the inspector or\nthe ACMA may retain it until:\n\n(a) the end of the period of 60 days after the seizure; or\n\n(b) if either of the following proceedings are instituted within\nthat period:\n\n(i) proceedings for an offence against, or arising out of, this\nAct in respect of which the thing may afford evidence;\n\n(ii) proceedings for a breach of the Spam Act 2003 in\nrespect of which the thing may afford evidence;\n\nthe proceedings (including any appeal to a court in relation to\nthose proceedings) are completed.\n\n(2) The ACMA may, by written instrument, authorise a thing seized\nunder this Division to be released to the owner, or to the person\nfrom whom it was seized, either:\n\n(a) unconditionally; or\n\n(b) on such conditions as the ACMA thinks fit, including\nconditions as to giving security for payment of its value if it\nis forfeited under section 551.\n\n(3) In this section:\nthis Act includes the Telecommunications (Consumer Protection\nand Service Standards) Act 1999 and regulations under that Act.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-546-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-546-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-546-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "2fe4c8998cff6bd946c8583eba22a6e143b0e1447f7e402ebd88179f24014a27"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-act-1997#sec-546",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "546 Retention of things seized",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 469,
      "values:originalText": "(1) If an inspector seizes a thing under this Division, the inspector or\nthe ACMA may retain it until:\n\n(a) the end of the period of 60 days after the seizure; or\n\n(b) if either of the following proceedings are instituted within\nthat period:\n\n(i) proceedings for an offence against, or arising out of, this\nAct in respect of which the thing may afford evidence;\n\n(ii) proceedings for a breach of the Spam Act 2003 in\nrespect of which the thing may afford evidence;\n\nthe proceedings (including any appeal to a court in relation to\nthose proceedings) are completed.\n\n(2) The ACMA may, by written instrument, authorise a thing seized\nunder this Division to be released to the owner, or to the person\nfrom whom it was seized, either:\n\n(a) unconditionally; or\n\n(b) on such conditions as the ACMA thinks fit, including\nconditions as to giving security for payment of its value if it\nis forfeited under section 551.\n\n(3) In this section:\nthis Act includes the Telecommunications (Consumer Protection\nand Service Standards) Act 1999 and regulations under that Act."
    },
