    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-547a",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "547A Powers available to inspectors for monitoring compliance",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-act-1997#sec-547a"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-act-1997.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-act-1997.pdf, p.471",
      "values:originalText": "(1) For the purpose of finding out whether the Spam Act 2003 has been\ncomplied with, an inspector may:\n\n(a) enter any premises; and\n\n(b) exercise the monitoring powers set out in section 547B.\n\n(2) An inspector is not authorised to enter premises under\nsubsection (1) unless:\n\n(a) the occupier of the premises has consented to the entry; or\n\n(b) the entry is made under a warrant under section 547D.\nConsent\n\n(3) Before obtaining the consent of a person for the purposes of\nparagraph (2)(a), the inspector must inform the person that he or\nshe may refuse consent.\n\n(4) An entry of an inspector by virtue of the consent of a person is not\nlawful unless the person voluntarily consented to the entry.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) For the purpose of finding out whether the Spam Act 2003 has been\ncomplied with, an inspector may:\n\n(a) enter any premises; and\n\n(b) exercise the monitoring powers set out in section 547B.\n\n(2) An inspector is not authorised to enter premises under\nsubsection (1) unless:\n\n(a) the occupier of the premises has consented to the entry; or\n\n(b) the entry is made under a warrant under section 547D.\nConsent\n\n(3) Before obtaining the consent of a person for the purposes of\nparagraph (2)(a), the inspector must inform the person that he or\nshe may refuse consent.\n\n(4) An entry of an inspector by virtue of the consent of a person is not\nlawful unless the person voluntarily consented to the entry.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-547a-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-547a-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-547a-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-act-1997-sec-547a-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "5a56d1a9ad32087dded6cf1f4bde299fd0e5ebf6ddca1eefd35cc3f9711c99f1"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-act-1997#sec-547a",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "547A Powers available to inspectors for monitoring compliance",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 471,
      "values:originalText": "(1) For the purpose of finding out whether the Spam Act 2003 has been\ncomplied with, an inspector may:\n\n(a) enter any premises; and\n\n(b) exercise the monitoring powers set out in section 547B.\n\n(2) An inspector is not authorised to enter premises under\nsubsection (1) unless:\n\n(a) the occupier of the premises has consented to the entry; or\n\n(b) the entry is made under a warrant under section 547D.\nConsent\n\n(3) Before obtaining the consent of a person for the purposes of\nparagraph (2)(a), the inspector must inform the person that he or\nshe may refuse consent.\n\n(4) An entry of an inspector by virtue of the consent of a person is not\nlawful unless the person voluntarily consented to the entry."
    },
